WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.772
Aliases and grain solve two different kinds of
confusion Aliases

00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:10.893
make the SQL easier to read Both patients and
admissions contain patient

00:00:10.919 --> 00:00:15.538
ID Writing p dot patient ID and a dot patient ID
tells

00:00:15.564 --> 00:00:19.919
us exactly which table a column comes from This
becomes even more

00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:25.302
important when a query joins three or four tables
Grain helps us

00:00:25.328 --> 00:00:31.186
interpret repeated values Suppose Alice has two
admissions Her

00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:35.462
patient name may appear on two rows after the join
That does

00:00:35.476 --> 00:00:39.318
not mean the join is wrong It means the result is at

00:00:39.344 --> 00:00:45.010
admission grain When you see repeated information
after a join do not

00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:51.078
immediately apply DISTINCT First ask whether those
rows represent different valid events

00:00:51.986 --> 00:00:57.197
DISTINCT can hide a modeling problem instead of
fixing it A good

00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:00.420
habit is to write a short sentence before the query
one row

00:01:00.447 --> 00:01:05.844
in this result represents one admission with patient
information That sentence becomes

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.226
your validation target
